 add('\nThe [2020 named write-in sheet](https://elections.delaware.gov/elections/resultsarchive/elect20/elect20_general/pdf/GE2020%20Official%20Write-In%20Votes-Statewide.pdf) supplies 17 presidential rows/336 votes statewide, without county/ED allocation. The [2022 sheet](https://elections.delaware.gov/elections/resultsarchive/elect22/elect22_general/GE2022_Official_WriteInVotes_Statewide.pdf) supplies **Edward Shlikas 3** and **Scott Walker 15** for House statewide, without county/ED allocation; its literal “2020 General Election” heading conflicts with November 8, 2022 and its November 11, 2022 7:23 PM clock. The [2024 sheet](https://elections.delaware.gov/elections/resultsarchive/elect24/elect24_general/GE2024_Official_WriteInVotes_Statewide.pdf) supplies 24 presidential county rows: **New Castle 942, Kent 148, Sussex 125; total 1,215**. It provides no ED allocation. All **43 candidate/method rows**, names, printed totals, blank cells and source page positions are retained in [writeins.csv](data/baselines/extracted/writeins.csv), with [word-position evidence](data/baselines/source/pdf-words.json) and [visual review](data/baselines/source/visual-review.json). Blank methods stay null. The 2020/2024 archived declared lists and tally sheets contain no Senate write-in entries; this does not establish a generic-write-in zero. Candidates appearing in a declared list without a tally row are not assigned fabricated zeros.')
 add('\n**All three counties, every comparison.** These are historical pair outcomes. New Castle favored A in all retained comparisons, Sussex favored B, and Kent changed from B in 2022 House to A in 2024 Senate/president. County other totals below are printed HTML candidates only. Full candidate JSON, supplemental scope, weights and separate denominator margins are in the [15-row county CSV](data/county-baselines.csv); all [2,699 state/county/RD/ED/Wilmington baseline rows](data/baselines/native-baselines.csv) remain available. Direct source tables are the official [2020](https://elections.delaware.gov/reports/GE2020.html), [2022](https://elections.delaware.gov/reports/GE2022.html) and [2024](https://elections.delaware.gov/reports/GE2024.html) reports.')
 add('\n| County | Contest | A | B | Named other | Named total | Pair margin pp |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|')
 for r in co:add(f"| {r['geo_key']} | {r['year']} {r['office_key']} | {i(r['A'])} | {i(r['B'])} | {i(r['named_other'])} | {i(r['named_total'])} | {100*float(r['pair_margin']):+.3f} |")
 add('\n**Conditional paths with complete weights.** For fixed pair votes `T_i=A_i+B_i`, `s_i=A_i/T_i`, `T=ΣT_i`, and `M=ΣT_i(2s_i−1)`, the raw uniform A-share shift for target pair margin `g` is `(gT−M)/(2T)`. At original weights, tie is `(B−A)/(2(A+B))`. A+1 pair-margin point sets `g=0.01` and adds **0.5 share points**, not one share point. A +1 share-point transfer adds **2 margin points** and **0.02T_i net votes** at fixed pair volume. For all-candidate denominator C, one margin point instead requires `0.01C` net votes: **5,006.06** in 2024 Senate. The A+1 pair cushion is only **0.960937 pp of the 500,606-vote all-candidate denominator**. These quantities are separate columns in [state scenarios](data/baselines/state-scenarios.csv) and [every-unit targets](data/baselines/unit-targets.csv).')
 add('\n| 2024 Senate county | Pair weight % | Historical A share % | A−B contribution | +1 share pp net gain | Uniform tie A share % | Uniform A+1 share % | Selected county alone: tie A share % |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|')
 for r in [r for r in co if r['year']=='2024' and r['office_key']=='USSenator']:
  tr=next(x for x in targets if x['scenario']=='2024-USSenator-bycounty-historical' and x['geo_key']==r['geo_key'] and x['target_pair_margin']=='0');cr=next(x for x in targets if x['scenario']==tr['scenario'] and x['geo_key']==r['geo_key'] and x['target_pair_margin']=='0.01')
  add(f"| {r['geo_key']} | {100*float(r['pair_state_weight']):.6f} | {100*float(r['pair_A_share']):.6f} | {int(r['net_A_minus_B']):+,} | {float(r['pair_gain_net_per_1pp']):,.2f} | {100*float(tr['target_A_share']):.6f} | {100*float(cr['target_A_share']):.6f} | {100*float(tr['selected_only_required_share']):.6f} |")
 add('\nSelected-only thresholds hold **every other county at its own unshifted 2024 Senate share and original pair volume**. For selected unit i, `q_i=[T_i+gT−M_other]/(2T_i)`. The full target file supplies the analogous result for every retained native unit/RD/alternative county view, with other-unit assumptions, weights, baseline/target net contributions, gains and both tie/+1 targets. They are alternative combinations, not necessary conditions in each county. Shares are explicitly clipped to [0,1] and a bounded monotone solver is used when the raw uniform shift is infeasible. Unbounded requirements, saturation solution rays, already-winning/secured, impossible and no-positive-pool states remain explicit. Zero-pair units remain rows with undefined share and zero weighted contribution.')
 add('\nDelaware’s legal general-election rule is **plurality over every individual candidate**, subject to a highest-vote tie; it is not a two-candidate majority, RCV or runoff. At the historical 2024 Senate pair tie, each major candidate would have 240,525.5 expectation votes while Katz remains 19,555 under that stated fixed-other assumption, so the illustrative pair calculation is relevant there. It does not guarantee a future plurality if another candidate’s votes change. No transfer/lower-preference or candidate-effect assumption is inferred. [15 Del. C. §5703 and tie provision §5708](https://delcode.delaware.gov/title15/c057/index.html).')
 add('\n**Historical volume, mix and preference sensitivity.** Low/base/high here are actual named-candidate county volume anchors: **2022 House 321,649 / 2024 Senate 500,606 / 2024 president 511,697**, respectively. Each county volume is multiplied by that county’s historical 2024 Senate pair participation `(A+B)/named_total`; the pair participation is retained in the settings. This simultaneously exposes historical county mix and volume differences. Unallocated 2022 House write-ins and separate 2024 presidential write-ins stay outside these expressly named-volume anchors. Preferences are independently anchored to each of 2020 Senate, 2022 House, 2024 Senate and 2024 president, giving 12 county combinations. These are historical sensitivities, **not low/base/high 2026 turnout forecasts**. [Exact settings](data/baselines/scenario-settings.json), [2022 primary volume source](https://elections.delaware.gov/reports/GE2022.html), [2024 primary volume/preference source](https://elections.delaware.gov/reports/GE2024.html), [2020 preference source](https://elections.delaware.gov/reports/GE2020.html).')
 add('\n| Historical volume / preference anchor | Pair volume | Unshifted net A−B | Tie shift: A share pp | A+1 shift: A share pp |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|')
 for r in ss:
  if r['scenario'].startswith('county-') and r['target_pair_margin']=='0':
   c=next(x for x in ss if x['scenario']==r['scenario'] and x['target_pair_margin']=='0.01');label=r['scenario'].replace('county-','').replace('-volume-',' / ').replace('-preference-',' / ').replace('RepresentativeinCongress','House').replace('PresidentandVicePresident','President').replace('USSenator','Senate');add(f"| {label} | {float(r['pair_volume']):,.2f} | {float(r['baseline_net']):+,.2f} | {float(r['bounded_shift_share_pp']):+.6f} | {float(c['bounded_shift_share_pp']):+.6f} |")
 add('\nThe native low/base/high sensitivities scale all 530 original 2024 Senate ED pair volumes by `321649/500606`, `1`, or `511697/500606`, preserving their original statewide native mix and preferences. County-mix sensitivities use stable counties; no cross-year ED equivalence is assumed. All nonpriority counties and native units remain in every state scenario. Different candidate effects, participation rates and remaining-vote assumptions require explicit new inputs; historical turnout does not estimate them.')
 add('\n**Native identities and exact priority.** Every available native office/table is inventoried. Requested contest ED coverage is **2024 Senate 530 / president 533; 2020 Senate 436 / president 439; 2022 House 516**. The headline 533/533 in 2024 cannot create Senate rows for presidential-only **0228/3117/4117**, whose printed presidential candidates are zero. In 2020 the extra presidential keys are **0218** (one Biden Machine vote), **3117/4117** (zero); no Senate identity is invented. The 2020 native Early Voting field stays null; state CSV early0 is a different publication scope. Every available candidate/method ED and RD sum reconciles to its independent statewide CSV. [Original 2024](https://elections.delaware.gov/reports/GE2024.html), [2020](https://elections.delaware.gov/reports/GE2020.html), [2022](https://elections.delaware.gov/reports/GE2022.html), [candidate/method checks](data/baselines/extracted/candidate-method-controls.csv).')
 add('\nCounty-office membership alone leaves special administrative ED parents unknown. Their parents are independently recovered, where supplied, from actual same-year participation nesting and the [official district structure](https://elections.delaware.gov/maps/pdfs/DEDistrictStructure_2022_2032_v4.pdf), updated February 13, 2024. Its title says 2022–2032 while the note says through the 2030 general. It explicitly labels 02-27/31-16/41-16 with footnote1 (15 Del. C. §1901(b)(3)), and 02-28/31-17/41-17 with footnote2 (42 USC1973aa-1). The [Department notice](https://elections.delaware.gov/candidates/pdfs/RegistrationTotalsPageInformation.pdf) explains federal-only overseas voters registered at county election-office addresses. These are administrative identities, not fabricated physical polling precincts or separate central absentee boards. Three 2020 presidential-only parents remain unavailable; their one/zero votes remain statewide without numeric-range inference. Methods are ED attributes; exhaustive central-board/site/style crosswalks and current boundaries are not established. [Resolved source evidence](data/baselines/extracted/resolved-parent-evidence.json), [inventory gaps](data/baselines/source/native-coverage-gaps.json).')
 add('\nThe `ByCounty` and `ByCountyW/Wilmington` report views are alternatives. New Castle’s inclusive totals already contain Wilmington. The latter view partitions Wilmington and Rest of New Castle; never add Wilmington to inclusive New Castle or combine those views. State/RD/ED/county totals also overlap. The arithmetic and source-row positions reconcile independently across both workers; source identities do not establish a cross-year or November 2026 geometry join. [2022 report views](https://elections.delaware.gov/reports/GE2022.html).')
 add('\nFor priority, the exact primary denominator is **500,606**, all three printed 2024 Senate candidates. County descending order is **New Castle 270,005; Sussex 144,333; Kent 86,268**. Minimum80% is the first two, **414,338 / 500,606 = 82.7673%**; the top15 contains the entire three-county universe, so the common-brief county union is **all three counties**. Native threshold is `ceil(4×500606/5)=400485`: first **310 EDs = 399,708**, first **311 = 400,539**; the crossing unit is literal **ElectionDistrict0807, 831 votes**. The top15 EDs contribute **35,875** votes. Full source-key ordering, every vote/weight and the exact integer crossing are in [ranking.csv](data/baselines/geography-ranking.csv) and [priority-contracts.json](data/baselines/priority-contracts.json). Tie order is lexical literal key. These are reconciled arithmetic contracts; the coordinator separately accepts legal/current geography.')
 add('\n| Independent native comparison | All printed candidates | EDs | Minimum80 ED count | Before crossing | At crossing | Top15 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|')
